{%- set st = schemas.v04CemExtensions -%}
{%- set ref = st.ref -%}
{%- set lifecycle = ref.definitions.LifecycleFields -%}
{%- set attribute = ref.definitions.AttributeExtensions -%}

{% from "schema-field-table.njk" import fieldTable with context %}

<section>
  <p class="eyebrow">Schemas / v0.4</p>
  <h1>Components</h1>
  <p class="lede">{{ ref.description }}</p>
  {% include "schema-version-nav.njk" %}
  <p>This is <strong>not</strong> the Custom Elements Manifest (CEM) schema
  itself, and it defines no manifest of its own — the fragment has no root
  scope to validate. It defines <em>extension</em> fields that a design
  system may add to CEM entries — declarations, attributes, members, slots,
  cssProperties, cssParts, and events — to record lifecycle metadata:
  status, deprecation details, and migration targets. Consumers apply the
  relevant definition alongside their pinned CEM schema.</p>
  <aside class="schema-install" aria-labelledby="use-components-schema">
    <div class="schema-install__intro">
      <h2 id="use-components-schema">Use these extensions</h2>
      <p>Reference a definition with <code>$ref</code> when composing a component manifest schema.</p>
      <p>The reference below is generated from the published schema and augmented with reader guidance.</p>
    </div>
    <dl class="schema-install__sources">
      <div><dt>Raw schema</dt><dd><a href="{{ st.canonicalUrl }}">{{ st.canonicalUrl }}</a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>npm</dt><dd><a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@designlasagna/schemas"><code>{{ st.npmImportPath }}</code></a></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Repository</dt><dd><a href="https://github.com/designlasagna/schemas">designlasagna/schemas</a></dd></div>
    </dl>
  </aside>
  <h2>Applying the extensions</h2>
  <p>The snippet below is a Custom Elements Manifest declaration with the
  extension fields applied: <code>status</code> and <code>deprecated</code> on
  the declaration, and a deprecated attribute value with its migration path.
  The fragment does not validate the surrounding CEM document, so this
  example is illustrative rather than validated against the fragment; the
  extension fields themselves follow the definitions below. Extension fields
  are additive — a valid CEM remains valid without them.</p>
  <pre class="json-example" tabindex="0"><code>{{ st.minimalExample | jsonCode | safe }}</code></pre>
</section>

<section id="lifecycle-fields">
  <h2>Lifecycle fields</h2>
  <p>The <code>LifecycleFields</code> definition is the composable set of
  optional lifecycle fields a design system may add to a CEM entry. The
  alias definitions below apply it at declaration, member, slot,
  cssProperty, cssPart, and event scope.</p>
  {{ fieldTable(lifecycle.properties, 'LifecycleFields', 'v04CemExtensions') }}
</section>

<section id="attribute-extensions">
  <h2>Attribute extensions</h2>
  <p>The <code>AttributeExtensions</code> definition extends a CEM attribute
  entry with its allowed value set, attribute-level lifecycle fields, and
  per-value deprecation records. Attribute-level lifecycle and
  <code>deprecatedValues</code> are independent axes.</p>
  {{ fieldTable(attribute.properties, 'AttributeExtensions', 'v04CemExtensions') }}
</section>

<section id="definitions">
  <h2>Nested definitions</h2>
  <p>Reusable shapes referenced by the fields above.</p>
  <div class="schema-definition-list">
    {% for name, definition in ref.definitions | dictsort %}
      {% if name != 'LifecycleFields' and name != 'AttributeExtensions' %}
      <article class="schema-definition">
        <h3 id="definition-{{ name }}"><code>{{ name }}</code></h3>
        {% set definitionGuide = schemaDocs.v04CemExtensions._definitions and schemaDocs.v04CemExtensions._definitions[name] or {} %}
        <p>{{ definitionGuide.summary or definition.description or 'See the raw schema for this definition.' }}</p>
        {% if definition.properties %}
          {{ fieldTable(definition.properties, name, 'v04CemExtensions') }}
        {% elif definition.$ref %}
          {% set refName = definition.$ref | schemaDefinitionName %}
          {% if refName %}
            <p>Alias for <a class="schema-ref" href="#definition-{{ refName }}"><code>{{ refName }}</code></a>.</p>
          {% else %}
            <p>Reference to <code>{{ definition.$ref }}</code> in the shared v0.4 <a href="{{ schemas.v04Lifecycle.canonicalUrl }}">lifecycle fragment</a>.</p>
          {% endif %}
        {% elif definition.type %}
          <p><code>{{ definition.type }}</code>
          {% if definition.description %} — {{ definition.description }}{% endif %}
          </p>
        {% endif %}
      </article>
      {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>

